From 1544acb7141843d457c4ba916d6aa3f0172ca50e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 16:10:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] parse: accept a path-shaped `:ty` fragment where a path is expected

sysinfo 0.33.1 src/common/mod.rs, `uid!(libc::uid_t, std::str::FromStr)` expanding
`xid!` with `$(, $trait:ty)?` into

    impl $trait for $name {
        type Err = <$type as $trait>::Err;
        ...
    }

    MACRO<::"sysinfo-0_33_1_H646090"::xid> error:0: Unexpected token
        TOK_INTERPOLATED_TYPE:std::str::FromStr, expected TOK_IDENT

`impl $trait for` already worked, because the impl header is parsed as a type and
then turned into a trait path. The trait in `<T as $trait>` goes through
`Parse_Path`, which accepted a `:path` fragment but not a `:ty` one. It fell
through to the relative-path branch, and that branch wants an identifier.

rustc's `parse_path` accepts an interpolated type when it is a plain path with no
qualified self (`TyKind::Path(None, _)`). `Parse_Path` now does the same, which is
the check `use $p;` already makes in root.cpp. Any other type fragment (`[u8]`,
`<T as Tr>::X`, ...) is still an "Unexpected token" error. No caller tries
`Parse_Path` and recovers from the error, so this only affects code that used to
fail to parse.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/parse/paths.cpp | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/parse/paths.cpp b/src/parse/paths.cpp
index ebaf999f..68b00e83 100644
--- a/src/parse/paths.cpp
+++ b/src/parse/paths.cpp
@@ -23,6 +23,13 @@ AST::Path Parse_Path(TokenStream& lex, eParsePathGenericMode generic_mode)
     {
     case TOK_INTERPOLATED_PATH:
         return mv$(tok.frag_path());
+    // A `:ty` fragment that is a plain path can be used as a path (same as rustc)
+    // - e.g. `<$type as $trait>::Err` with `$trait:ty` (sysinfo 0.33.1)
+    case TOK_INTERPOLATED_TYPE:
+        if( !tok.frag_type().is_path() || tok.frag_type().path().m_class.is_UFCS() ) {
+            throw ParseError::Unexpected(lex, tok);
+        }
+        return mv$(tok.frag_type().path());
 
     case TOK_RWORD_SELF:
         GET_CHECK_TOK(tok, lex, TOK_DOUBLE_COLON);
